WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.090
Narrator: Broadway shows
don't last forever.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.320
But some of the costumes used
on stage can get a second act,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.220
thanks to a not-for-profit organization

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.680
called the TDF Costume Collection.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.950
Cabral: We have a dress from
"Wicked," worn by Elphaba,

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.010
as well as one of the
"Mamma Mia!" jumpsuits.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.070
Narrator: That's Stephen
Cabral, the director

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.700
of the collection, which is
based in Astoria, Queens.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.860
TDF accepts donations from
various theatrical performances

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.640
at the end of their run and rents them

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.470
back out to other performances for reuse.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:39.530
Cabral: We do approximately
1,000 productions a year,

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.959
renting over 10,000 costumes,

00:00:41.959 --> 00:00:46.090
both here in New York City
as well as nationwide.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:47.970
Narrator: They have over 80,000 items

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.511
in their collection,
including shoes, shirts,

00:00:50.511 --> 00:00:55.510
dresses, hats, gloves, and
various other accessories.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.793
Cabral: A lot of times
when a Broadway show

00:00:57.793 --> 00:01:00.376
will eventually close,

00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:05.240
the costumes are usually
retained for the tour or backup.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.007
But every once in a while,
a Broadway show will say,

00:01:07.007 --> 00:01:10.250
"Okay, we're done with these
clothes; you can have them."

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.679
Narrator: TDF started
back in the early '70s,

00:01:12.679 --> 00:01:15.580
when the Metropolitan Opera
moved to Lincoln Center

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.920
and donated costumes from 22 shows.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.010
TDF has since expanded,
offering all sorts of items

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.410
donated from a variety of
performances and individuals.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.468
Cabral: Recently, we
received a rather large

00:01:28.468 --> 00:01:30.200
and very exciting donation.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.620
Hollywood, Broadway fashion
designer, Bob Mackie.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.300
Narrator: Some notable pieces
Mackie donated were a dress

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.744
worn by Julia Louis Dreyfuss
at the 1997 Emmy awards

00:01:40.744 --> 00:01:42.357
and an entire production

00:01:42.357 --> 00:01:45.120
of "The Best Little
Whorehouse Goes Public."

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.203
Cabral: When the original
production of "Rent" 

00:01:47.211 --> 00:01:48.153
finally closed,

00:01:48.153 --> 00:01:50.280
after running for many, many years,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.070
we were very fortunate to receive

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.290
all of the original
costumes from the original

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.160
off-Broadway production,
before it moved to Broadway.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.866
We received many donations over the years

00:01:59.866 --> 00:02:02.940
from "Mamma Mia!" when
"Mamma Mia!" was on Broadway.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.681
We have a dress worn by Jane Krakowski

00:02:04.681 --> 00:02:08.290
from a recent production
of "She Loves Me,"

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:10.665
some costumes worn by Patti Lupone

00:02:10.665 --> 00:02:12.356
in the revival of "Gypsy."

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.086
Narrator: While many of
the items are iconic,

00:02:14.086 --> 00:02:16.320
you won't necessarily find something worn

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.483
by big celebrities like
Cher or Barbara Streisand.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.448
Cabral: Many celebrities
actually wanna hold on

00:02:23.448 --> 00:02:24.281
to their costumes,

00:02:24.286 --> 00:02:28.320
either to potentially open
a museum or more likely

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.380
later on in life have some
sort of celebrity auction.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.120
Narrator: Many amateur stage
performances rent from TDF,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.990
but so do some TV shows and movies.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.180
Cabral: "Saturday Night
Live," when they're filming,

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.890
depending on their season and their needs,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.470
is in here sometimes every week.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:44.900
Narrator: TDF costumes can be seen

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.470
in this skit with Betty White.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.634
"Now all we need is a
dress and some proper shoes

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.183
and a little womanly allure."

00:02:50.183 --> 00:02:52.550
Narrator: And this skit with
Justin Timberlake as Mozart.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.848
Both "Birdman" and "12 Years a Slave"

00:02:54.848 --> 00:02:57.270
featured costumes in the film

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.450
that were rented here from
the TDF Costume Collection.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.633
Cabral: The only type of
rental that we really don't do

00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:02.466
is Halloween.

00:03:02.466 --> 00:03:04.890
Our costumes are really
not Halloween-worthy.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.110
We also try to avoid doing rentals

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.360
where there is any food
or beverage involved.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.338
Narrator: Anyone can go in
and explore the collection.

00:03:11.338 --> 00:03:13.190
But if you wanna try anything on,

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:15.440
you have to fill out some paperwork first.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.374
They have mannequins you
can use to check sizes

00:03:17.374 --> 00:03:19.720
and dressing rooms you
can book in advance.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:20.963
"Here I go!"

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.940
Narrator: Cost is based on a whole look,

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.620
not per individual item.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.928
Cabral: The other part
of our pricing exists 

00:03:28.928 --> 00:03:29.803
with number of weeks

00:03:29.811 --> 00:03:32.190
of performances and how
large your theater is.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:33.110
Narrator: You also have to pay

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:35.880
to have the costumes dry
cleaned before returning them.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.270
Stephen says they have a
green strategy in place

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.370
to make sure all the items
get as much use as possible.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.690
Once an item gets too ragged,
it's moved to a special area,

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.720
where these types of items are in demand.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.200
Cabral: We have a distressed section.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.306
So for productions like
"Les Mis" or "Urinetown,"

00:03:51.306 --> 00:03:54.500
these are costumes that
need to not look so great.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.740
Narrator: And two or three times a year,

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.270
they open up the warehouse
for a special bag sale.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.170
Cabral: You pay for that bag, you go in,

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.536
and whatever you can shove into that bag

00:04:01.536 --> 00:04:04.700
and take out the door is yours.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.719
Narrator: So no matter what
condition a costume is in,

00:04:06.719 --> 00:04:10.013
there's always a chance
at an encore performance.

00:04:11.292 --> 00:04:12.990
Cabral: We sometimes will put costumes

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.580
on people and there's nothing.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.060
Nothing changes, they're
just kind of like, "Eh."

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.270
Then you put costumes on people

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:22.800
and it brings out a whole
other personality or two.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.053
And it's kind of fun.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:27.656
"Lovely to meet you."

